 "diagnostics": {
  "cosine_distance_broken": {
   "finding": "cos-dist(agent 20px from pellet, goal) = 0.0000; at 3px = 0.081. Non-monotonic, ~zero everywhere.",
   "cause": "pellet+wall features dominate latent DIRECTION; agent position affects norm/small subspace. Cosine normalizes the signal away.",
   "implication": "GWMR's cosine-to-goal cost requires hyperspherical latents (their SIGReg). tiny-jepa has no geometric regularization."
  },
  "l2_weak_gradient": {
   "finding": "On REAL latents, L2 is monotonic with distance (probe: 0.07@20px -> 3.83@3px), but in practice moving 12px toward pellet changes L2-to-goal only 6.43 vs 6.25 (away).",
   "cause": "Goal embedding dominated by static features; agent-position component is small relative to total latent norm."
  },
  "predictor_inverts_signal": {
   "finding": "Imagined 'toward pellet' vs 'away': correct ordering only 3/5 seeds (chance). Seed 5000: toward=9.12, away=5.56 (inverted).",
   "cause": "Predictor was never trained to preserve goal-directedness; imagined latents are not rank-consistent with true outcomes."
  },
  "predictor_drift": {
   "finding": "imagined-vs-real latent L2 grows 1.19 -> 3.21 over 6 rollout steps."
  }
 },
 "honest_verdict": "Latent-space goal planning FAILS on this encoder, for three compounding reasons: (1) cosine distance is meaningless without hyperspherical geometry (GWMR had SIGReg; tiny-jepa doesn't); (2) even with L2, the goal gradient is weak because static features dominate the latent; (3) the predictor inverts or scrambles the goal signal in imagination (3/5 correct ordering = chance) and drifts 1.2->3.2 over 6 steps. This BOUNDS but does not refute the GWMR diagnosis: having wall information in latents (proven: 3.2x probe) is necessary but NOT sufficient \u2014 the metric structure of the latent space must also support planning. Fix directions, in order: (a) geometric regularization (SIGReg-style) during encoder training so distances are meaningful; (b) predictor training that preserves goal-directedness; (c) longer horizon. The planner code is clean and correct (CEM optimizes whatever score it's given \u2014 elite means decreased every run); the score itself carries no plannable signal.",
